# OpenSCAD parameters (agent authoring)

These are **authoring conventions for agents**, not part-design Blueprint rules. Apply to every parametric `.scad` part unless the user directs otherwise.

## Customizer parameter split

Split top-level assignments into:

1. **User-facing named groups** — `/* [GroupName] */` for values a user is likely to change in Customizer.
2. **`/* [Hidden] */`** — design constants and uncommon knobs; still named top-level assignments (no magic numbers inside modules).

- Order user groups by concern (e.g. Objects → Export → what is being sized → functional features → enclosure/body → mounts), not by implementation order.
- Put **derived** values after parameters; do not expose them in user groups.
- Edit Hidden values in the `.scad` when tuning the design.

## Objects checkboxes

- Every Blueprint `###` object has one Customizer checkbox, `show_<section_name>`, default `true`, in a user-facing `/* [Objects] */` group. `### cap` means `show_cap`. The identifier matches the heading exactly.
- That checkbox wraps the object's whole module. Off means none of that object's solids are in the model. It must not hide another object's geometry, and it must not leave a piece of its own behind.
- Do not add a show-checkbox for a sub-feature. Only Blueprint objects get one.
- Renaming an object renames its checkbox in the same change.
- Geometry that exists without a confirmed Blueprint section may keep a preview checkbox only until that section is confirmed; do not treat it as a specified object for export.

## Object preview colors

- Every object instance in the design is wrapped with `preview_object(color_index_<name>)` from `lib/fdm.scad` so F5 shows each object in a different color.
- Assign a **stable** integer `color_index_<name>` in `/* [Hidden] */` (or next to the Objects group). Do not renumber when editing geometry — the index is the persistent identity of that object’s color.
- Use the shared `preview_palette` in `lib/fdm.scad`. Do not reorder or replace palette entries; new hues are appended only. Indices wrap with `% len(preview_palette)`.
- Color comes from OpenSCAD `color()` and shows in Cursor preview only when `openscad.previewFormat` is `"3mf"` (STL preview is monochrome). Desktop OpenSCAD shows it with **F5** (not F6). Exported STL stays uncolored; 3MF can keep colors when OpenSCAD writes them.

```scad
/* [Hidden] */
color_index_barrel     = 0;
color_index_cap        = 1;
color_index_cartridge  = 2;

if (show_barrel)
    preview_object(color_index_barrel)
        pen_barrel();
```

## Export format dropdown

Every design `.scad` always has:

```scad
/* [Export] */
export_format = "STL"; // [STL, 3MF]
```

- Always present, even for a one-object design. Options are only `STL` and `3MF`.
- It does not change the preview solid. OpenSCAD CLI and the Makefile choose the file type from this value (output extension `.stl` or `.3mf`).
- `make exports` / `make part` read the design's `export_format` default, or override with `FORMAT=STL` / `FORMAT=3MF`.

## Per-object mesh export

Slicers must see each Blueprint object as its own printable part. Preview may show several objects together; export does not weld them.

- Export one mesh file per Blueprint object into `exports/`: `exports/<design-path>/<design>_<object>.<ext>`. STL and 3MF share that tree.
- Each export run enables only that object's `show_*`; other objects are off so they are not in the mesh.
- An object may be several disconnected solids in its own file when that is how it prints. Different Blueprint objects must not share one file.
- Do not `union()` unrelated Blueprint objects into one solid for export. Assembly preview is separate top-level `if (show_*)` calls, not one fused body.
- Format is the container (STL or 3MF); it does not put every object into a single multi-body file.

## User-facing names

OpenSCAD shows identifiers with underscores as spaces — choose names that read as plain labels.

- Prefer full words over abbreviations: `corner_radius` not `fillet`/`r`, `floor_thickness` not `floor_t`.
- Name by **what the user is adjusting**, not internal implementation.
- Trailing comment on every parameter: `// mm — short plain-language meaning` (or `deg — …`).

```scad
// ❌ BAD
/* [Body] */
fillet = 1.2;  // mm
t      = 2.0;  // mm

// ✅ GOOD
/* [Body] */
corner_radius   = 1.2;  // mm — outer corner radius (plan view)
wall_thickness  = 2.0;  // mm — outer wall thickness
```

## Hidden / derived

- Clear named assignments; slightly shorter names OK if the comment stays unambiguous.
- Derived: compute after parameters; never as Customizer-facing groups.
